---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import Steps from "../../components/article/Steps.astro";
import Benefits from "../../components/article/Benefits.astro";
import Note from "../../components/article/Note.astro";
import { Clock, Shield, FileText, Search } from "lucide-react";
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="Private Client Meeting Notes for Financial Advisors on Mac | Summit"
  description="Client meeting notes for financial advisors on your Mac, with no bot joining the call. Local transcription and summaries; optional cloud AI is your choice."
  audience="Finance Professionals"
  current="finance"
  heading="Client meeting notes for financial advisors, kept on your Mac"
  lead="Reviews, planning sessions and discovery calls each need a clear record you can come back to. Summit turns recorded conversations into transcripts and summaries on your Mac, with folders to organize clients and search to revisit what was discussed."
  ctaHeading="Try Summit on your next client meeting"
  ctaText="Summit runs on macOS. Choose Local Models in AI settings to keep summaries and chat on your Mac."
>
  <h2 class="!mt-0">You choose where client conversations are processed</h2>
  <p>
    Transcription runs on your Mac, and there is no Summit account holding your
    meeting library. Select Local Models and summaries and chat run on your Mac
    too. If you choose a cloud AI provider, it receives transcript excerpts and,
    for chat, your question with related topics, action items and meeting titles
    and dates, so review that setting before recording client conversations.
    Markdown auto-export and iCloud sync are off until you turn them on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recording consent, your firm's policies and your regulatory obligations
    still apply. The <a href="/security/architecture">architecture page</a>
    lists exactly what is stored and what can leave your Mac.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep each client's context separate in your workflow</h2>
  <Steps
    steps={[
      {
        title: "Create a folder for each client or household",
        body: 'Give every relationship its own folder, and optionally a folder <a href="/docs/guides/summary-templates">summary template</a> so reviews and planning sessions are written up the same way.',
      },
      {
        title: "Use clear meeting titles",
        body: "Name meetings with the client and meeting type, so the right conversation is easy to spot in search results.",
      },
      {
        title: "Check the scope before you ask",
        body: "Filter search by folder, or select that client's meetings and chat about just those. The chat shows which meetings are included before you ask a question.",
      },
      {
        title: "Open the original passage",
        body: "Chat answers cite timestamped transcript passages. Check the source before copying a goal, figure or decision into your CRM or a client follow-up.",
      },
    ]}
  />
  <Note>
    Folders help organize notes; they are not a separate account, encrypted
    vault or access-control boundary.
  </Note>

  <h2>Notes, not a compliance archive</h2>
  <p>
    Summit is a note-taking tool. It is not a books-and-records system,
    communications archive or supervision tool, and using it does not by itself
    meet SEC, FINRA, MiFID or other recordkeeping requirements. Check your
    firm's recordkeeping and supervision rules before recording client meetings,
    and decide where any notes you keep as official records should live.
  </p>
  <p>
    Summaries are a starting point, not a guarantee that every detail was
    captured. Use the original transcript to check amounts, dates and
    commitments.
  </p>

  <h2>Why finance professionals choose Summit</h2>
  <Benefits
    benefits={[
      {
        icon: Clock,
        title: "Start from a draft",
        body: "Summit drafts a summary with key points and action items after each recording, so your meeting notes start from a draft instead of a blank page.",
      },
      {
        icon: Shield,
        title: "No meeting bot",
        body: "Summit records from your Mac instead of joining the call as a participant, for video calls and in-person meetings alike.",
      },
      {
        icon: FileText,
        title: "Consistent meeting write-ups",
        body: "Summary templates shape notes for annual reviews, discovery meetings and planning sessions.",
      },
      {
        icon: Search,
        title: "Recall with sources",
        body: "Search past meetings for a client's goals, concerns or earlier decisions, and follow chat citations back to the passage in the transcript.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Who it's for</h2>
  <h3>Financial advisors &amp; wealth managers</h3>
  <p>
    Take notes from discovery meetings, quarterly reviews and planning sessions,
    then find what a client said about goals or concerns before the next review.
  </p>
  <h3>Investment analysts</h3>
  <p>
    Keep notes from management calls, investor briefings and research
    discussions, and search them when you revisit a thesis.
  </p>
  <h3>Financial planners</h3>
  <p>
    Record intake sessions and draft summaries of goals and circumstances, then
    check the transcript before building the plan.
  </p>
  <h3>Private bankers</h3>
  <p>
    Keep a folder for each relationship so earlier discussions about lending,
    estate planning or investments are easy to find.
  </p>
</UseCaseLayout>
